\section{Estimation}{

The latent values are integrated out by the Laplace approximation,
together with the random effects. For a gaussian response with a
linear \code{me()} term the marginal likelihood is multivariate normal,
and the approximation is exact. An interaction of two \code{me()} terms,
or a non-gaussian response, makes it approximate.

Several \code{me()} terms that share a grouping have correlated latent
values by default, as in brms. Use \code{\link[=set_mecor]{set_mecor()}} to make them
independent.

\code{me()} can appear on its own, in a \code{:} or \code{*} interaction with
numeric variables or with other \code{me()} terms, and in the formula of
any distributional or nonlinear parameter. It cannot appear in a
group-level term, in a nonlinear formula body, inside another
function call, or in the same interaction as \code{mo()} or \code{mi()}.
